# Browser sign-in persistence: implementation and verification

The original failure was reproduced before changing the product: planning create/update tools returned credential-free task URLs, but reopening either after the last signed-in tab closed produced Unauthorized. Authentication lived in tab storage. The regression now exercises that same sequence and compares its complete output with the original expected output.

## Delivered behavior

- Better Auth 1.7.6 owns sign-in token issuance, verification and sessions. SQLite uses Node 24's built-in driver. The database, signing secret and separate launcher/tool credential stay in the machine-local runtime store's `auth/` directory, outside versioned buildspace artifacts.
- The launcher supplies a single-use sign-in link valid for two minutes. The browser consumes it, removes it from the address and receives a host-only HttpOnly cookie, with a 30-day absolute session lifetime. No session credential is exposed through page JavaScript, Web Storage, task links or screenshot URLs.
- Read, create and update tool links reopen without another signed-in tab. The cookie and database also survive browser and server restarts. Expired sessions require opening the launcher again; clearing browser data also requires sign-in again.
- The central API gate verifies sessions and exact Host/Origin policy. It rejects cross-origin actions, invalid/expired/revoked credentials, and anonymous credential issuance. Local tools have a separate library-issued session. Open event streams terminate on expiry or revocation.
- The main application uses `localhost`; managed previews use separate loopback IP hosts and separate auth stores. Cookies are not isolated by port, so distinct hosts are required. Existing `127.0.0.1` bookmarks redirect to the canonical host while preserving the task selection.
- The launcher can open an already running app with a fresh link. Server shutdown handles requests still authenticating and unused browser preconnection sockets without hanging.
- The full checkpoint exposed a navigation race: a delayed queue-control response could switch back to Task items after the user opened Feed. Queue and trigger controls now respect the existing render-validity contract. A controlled delayed-response regression fails before the fix and passes afterwards; the queue-review and scheduled-execution workflows also pass.

## Verification

The security and persistence regression tests cover cookie attributes, one-use links, invalid credentials, Host/Origin rejection, expiry, revocation, separate preview hosts, browser restart and server restart. Additional lifecycle regressions cover late-authenticated requests during shutdown, unused preconnection sockets and repeated launcher use.

In the actual Codex browser, all Broccoli tabs were closed and the tab inventory was empty. Opening the tool-returned design-task URL then showed the exact task, without another sign-in. Opening its old `127.0.0.1` URL redirected to `localhost` with the correct task selected. The authenticated page also survived the subsequent app restart.

Full repository checkpoint: `npm run test:all` exited successfully. All 135 backend tests and all 108 browser tests passed. The browser suite includes both create/update-link regressions and the controlled queue-navigation regression. Syntax and whitespace checks passed for the affected source. A subsequent tool-help wording correction documents that a valid browser session survives closed tabs; it does not change behavior.

## Review boundary

This secures the local browser/API transport. It is not per-person/buildspace authorization, encryption of workspace files, or isolation from programs running as the same operating-system user. Managed previews are host-isolated; arbitrary software hosted on `localhost` is not an OS security sandbox. Separate MCP transport authentication remains unchanged.

The changes are available in the running app and remain uncommitted. The existing MVP review has not been accepted or marked complete.
